+- 2026-10-14: C2ME idea 3: chunk files are written by an I/O thread (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; +1 test `chunks::tests::load_prefers_the_write_still_queued`, not run; `edits_survive_a_restart` must still pass: it flushes with `usize::MAX`, which now waits for the thread). Source: `c2me-rewrites-chunkio` (chunk I/O off the main thread); picked as the easiest of the remaining ideas to write, and it removes the file writes (`fs::write` + `rename`, an fsync-less but still blocking syscall pair per chunk) from the render thread's autosave and unload paths.
+    - Before: `ChunkManager::flush` (autosave, pause, exit) and the unload step of `update` called `write_entry` = `encode_chunk` + `save::write` on the render thread, one atomic file write per dirty chunk. Now `Writer` (`chunk-io` thread, one FIFO) does the `save::write`; the render thread only runs `encode_chunk` (RLE, ~48 KB in, ~5 KB out) and queues the bytes. `with_dir` starts the thread; without a save dir nothing is written, as before.
+    - What keeps it correct: (1) `Writer.inflight` holds the newest queued bytes per chunk until that write is answered, and `load` reads them before the file, so a chunk unloaded and walked back to before its write landed keeps its edits (the file is still the older one then); (2) `flush(usize::MAX)` (pause and exit, `lib.rs` `save(usize::MAX)`) waits until every sent write is answered (`pending` counts them), so the process may end right after; (3) a failed write is answered back: the chunk is dirty again if still loaded (the next `flush` retries), one log line per reap; one already unloaded is lost, as it was when the write ran inline; (4) `saved` is set when the write is queued, so the chunk loads from `inflight` / the file instead of being generated again.
+    - ponytail: the encode stays on the render thread (the upgrade is to send clones of the arrays and encode on `chunk-io`, +48 KB of copy per chunk); `level` and `decor` files are still written inline by `save()` (one small file each); no write coalescing beyond "newest bytes win in `inflight`"; the thread stops with the manager. UNVERIFIED on a device: the saved frame-time, which depends on the storage (the old cost was a handful of ~5 KB files per border crossing).
+- 2026-10-14: M21 part 5, piston animation (compiled with `cargo check`, `cargo test --lib` run in a Linux scratch crate that holds `world` + `render` + `lib.rs` with `android_activity` stubbed: 162 pass, the 14 that fail also fail without this patch: stale `chunks(6)` strides against `VF = 8` in the mesh / outline tests, shaders that need a GPU or `sm_uv`, `climate`, `decor::paintings_fall_off_and_are_picked`). `TileEntityPiston` is a `ticks::Mv` inside a `Falling` record (`Falling.mv`, `Falling.meta`): `Ticks::piece` writes the invisible moving block 36 into the destination cell and starts a piece that slides `id` / `meta` half a block per tick, so 2 ticks (`TileEntityPiston.onUpdate`); `fall` finishes it in order (a retracting head clears its cell before the pulled block fills it) and puts `then` there, a head whose base let go meanwhile is dropped (`head_wanted`). The pushed blocks, the head sliding out, the head sliding back and the sticky pull are all pieces; the base takes its bit 8 at once. Drawn by `render::items` with the block's own `box_bounds` (head plate, trimmed base) and its flat tile; 36 is skipped by the chunk mesher and has no collision. `lib.rs` carries the player and the mobs inside a piece along half a block per tick (`TileEntityPiston.func_31010_a`; ponytail: no collision check on the way, dropped items are not carried). The test `pistons_push_and_the_sticky_one_pulls` now checks the 36 cells and the 2 tick delay. Still no shaft model, no sound.
+- 2026-10-14: M22 part 1, rails (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; +4 tests `rail::tests::*`, +1 `craft::tests::rail_recipes_match_java`). New `world/rail.rs` ports `RailLogic` (the join graph of cells, `refresh`, `can_connect`, `connect_to`, `place`) and `BlockRail.onNeighborBlockChange` / `func_27044_a` (the powered-rail chain) against a small `Rails` trait (implemented for `ChunkManager`, and for a toy grid in the tests). `ticks.rs`: `added` (27 / 28 / 66) lays the shape and bends the neighbours, `neighbour` drops a rail with no ground or slope support and re-evaluates power, `stay` has the ground rule for `canPlaceBlockAt`; `craft.rs`: rails (16), powered rails (6), detector rails (6). ponytail: the detector rail is never switched on (no minecarts yet); a changed rail's own metadata write pings its neighbours like any notifying write (vanilla's `requiresSelfNotify` for rails differs only in who hears it); a plain rail is re-laid on a power change only through the `power` flag the notify loop already computes.
 - 2026-10-14: C2ME idea 2: the light engine does a block update's work with far fewer lookups (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; +1 test `light::tests::relit_matches_the_plain_formula`, not run; `light_settles_after_edits` is the settled-result check and must still pass). Chosen as the best lever for block-update speed: every edit (`set_quiet` -> `light_after_set`, flowing water, TNT, tree growth) queues light regions, and `mesh_pending` only queues a mesh job while `light_queue` is empty, so the light engine's throughput is how fast a block update becomes visible. Source: the allocation / lookup trimming side of C2ME (`c2me-opts-allocs`); the threaded light engine (`c2me-threading-lighting`) is NOT done: it needs mutable access to the chunk light arrays across borders, a rewrite of `update_region` / `init_chunk` that is too risky to write blind.
     - Cost found by reading `world/chunks/light.rs`: a cell of `update_region` read its own light, block, open-sky test and six neighbours with ~9 `HashMap` lookups; each CHANGED cell added `set_saved_light` (`chunk_exists` + `put_light` + `mark_dirty`, which built a `Vec` per call: a heap allocation) and 3-6 `neighbor_changed` (`chunk_exists` + `sees_sky` / `raw_block` + `saved_light` + `schedule_light`'s own `chunk_exists`): ~30-40 lookups and one allocation per changed cell. `run_light` also read `Instant::now()` after EVERY region, most of which are one cell.
     - Now: `relit` reads a cell from its chunk entry once (the neighbours inside the same chunk straight from its arrays, the ones across a border through `saved_light` as before); `neighbor_changed` is one lookup; `set_saved_light` has no extra `chunk_exists`; `mark_dirty` fills a 3-key array instead of a `Vec`; `run_light` reads the clock after a region of more than 256 cells or every 32nd region. Nothing else changed: the light values, their order of evaluation and the queue are the same, so the settled light is the same (`relit_matches_the_plain_formula` compares it with the old formula on borders, corners and the world's top and bottom).
